


@@BEGIN: PRESENTATION
@id: desmond
@title: Aims and design principles of the BROKENSONG digital edition
@presenter: desmond
@abstract: 

Most Insular polyphony of the later Middle Ages is in a
fragmentary and incomplete state. Just under 140 Insular
manuscript sources of polyphony are extant from the period
c.1150-c.1350. Only one is a complete codex of polyphony;
32 Insular miscellanies and liturgical books have isolated
polyphonic compositions entered as additions to their main
contents. Otherwise about 280 leaves are extant from just
over a hundred books or rolls of polyphony, most of which
were reused as binding materials for other books (as
flyleaves, pastedowns, or other binding reinforcements).
The total number of Insular compositions in these c.140
Insular manuscripts totals just over 800 polyphonic
compositions.

Due to its fragmentary survival, a large portion of this
repertoire is little known. The primary critical edition
are the four volumes (vols. 14-17) published from 1979 to
1986 in the series <i>Polyphonic Music of the Fourteenth
Century</i> (hereafter PMFC). For these volumes, the editors
made the decision that, for the most part, they would not
edit compositions that survived incompletely. According to
tallies given in the apparatus to the four PMFC volumes,
approximately 44% percent of the Insular repertoire then
known to the editors was left unedited, and that percentage
is even higher today given the continued discovery of new
polyphonic fragments.

One of the primary outputs of the ERC-BROKENSONG project
(PI: Desmond) is an innovative ‘born-digital’ scholarly
edition that will eventually present editions and
reconstructions of the complete Insular polyphonic corpus
c.1150-c.1350, but has begun with the fragmentary unedited
corpus. This corpus poses many challenges for including
illegibility due to damage and that voice parts and entire
sections may be lost. This paper will outline the aims and
design principles for the BROKENSONG digital edition that
will allow users to search and browse by source,
composition, notation and genre and access text
translations, playback MIDI, view the compositions in
medieval or modern notation, and also view images of the
manuscript sources, including new multispectral images of
any damaged leaves. Principles of encoding the medieval
notation in MEI and issues relating to the display,
storage, citation and sustainability of the individual
editions will also be discussed.


@@END: PRESENTATION



